    "question": "<p>1. A set is a data structure which helps us to collect items/objects of the same type/domain. An ordered set is one whose position of its elements is relevant. Consider the following structure for an ordered set:</p><pre class=\"sheet\">struct OrderedSet{\n       int data[MAX_SIZE]; // store elements\n       int size;\n\n};</pre><p>Propose the code snippet(s) ONLY that is at position (a) and (b) for the methods to initialize an ordered set (initSet()) or check if an ordered set contains some element (contains()). Initializing a set means deleting/clearing all its elements/data, while “contains” is to check if a value is an element of the set. (2+3 = 5 marks)</p><pre class=\"sheet\">void initSet(OrderedSet *set) {\n       ___(a)___\n\n}\n\nbool contains(OrderedSet *set, int value) {\n       for (int i = 0; i &lt; set-&gt;size; i++) {\n               ___(b)___\n       }\n       return false;\n\n}</pre>",
